{
  "abstract": "For every integer $x\\ge2$, the shortcut Collatz orbit and its observed multiplicative coefficients agree in first-exit date and direction from $[x,9x/2]$ and $[1,9/2]$, respectively, through $P=217976794617$ steps. For $1\\le n\\le P$, exactly $2^{n-\\lceil n\\log_3 2\\rceil}$ classes modulo $2^n$ survive through step $n$. Fixed-date comparisons at sufficiently large starts give an irrational natural density of upward first exits, $0.0846764346764346903\\ldots$, with the complementary density for downward exits. For $2\\le x<2^{100}$ the sharp first-exit bound is 164 steps, uniquely attained downward. The latest upward first exit is at step 153. All $1048659$ starts in that range surviving through step 126 descend below their own start by step 382 and enter Bařina's verified interval below $2^{71}$ by step 701. The maxima occur at different starts. Hence a nonconvergent orbit with minimum $m$ in that range must exceed $9m/2$ within 126 steps from $m$. Arbitrary internal swaps in a mechanical word give $2^{80448749304}$ residue classes that first descend at $P$. At the critical counts, proximity to low-complexity templates forces descent with a quantitative decrement under protection. A protected segment with a nondecreasing endpoint has at least $30346365$ disjoint 176-step windows containing $11$ and having height ratio above $3^{55}/2^{85}$. The supplement replays the exact searches, arithmetic and downstream traces.",
